---
description: Keep RockMobile source modular and avoid growing god files
alwaysApply: true
---

# RockMobile module boundaries

- Keep every source file focused on one cohesive responsibility. Do not grow a mixed UI,
  state, networking, persistence, navigation, and test implementation into one large file.
- When a change adds an independent responsibility, extract it immediately into a clearly
  named module or feature folder. Preserve existing public APIs and navigation behavior
  through thin facades or compatibility exports where callers rely on them.
- Separate API DTO/client code, domain models and validation, state/controllers, UI screens
  and reusable components, persistence, and tests when they are independently readable and
  testable. Colocate tests with their feature when a production file becomes hard to scan.
- Prefer simple data classes/structs and direct composition. Introduce an interface, trait,
  factory, or generic abstraction only at a real boundary with multiple implementations or
  a necessary fake; do not add architecture for one implementation.
- Before adding substantial code to an already large file, inspect callers and nearby
  features, then choose the smallest coherent extraction. Preserve UI behavior, account
  ownership, API contracts, error handling, and offline behavior during structural refactors.
- Add a brief module-level description for a nontrivial feature: responsibility, key
  invariant, and intentional exclusions. Do not duplicate implementation in comments.
- Keep an existing source architecture map accurate when module boundaries or recommended
  reading order materially change; create one only when the project has enough modules to
  benefit from it.

## JSON and API boundaries

- Do not hand-build JSON strings, maps, trees, or JSON-path parsing in application code.
  Define strict request/response/event DTOs and use the project's typed serializer.
- Decode every server payload into a validated typed model at the API boundary. UI, state,
  and domain code must consume typed values, not raw JSON, dynamic maps, or untyped fields.
- Model nullable, optional, unknown, and forward-compatible values explicitly. Reject or
  safely ignore malformed/unknown wire variants at the boundary; never guess missing fields.
- Hand-written JSON is allowed only in a narrowly scoped test fixture when no typed fixture
  helper can express an intentionally malformed payload; state that intent in the test.
